# REEFER storefront — PRODUCTION build config. Read by `npm run build`, which is
# what GitHub Actions runs on every push to main before rsyncing dist/ to Hostinger
# (https://reeferclothing.com). Vite inlines this value INTO the bundle at build
# time, so it is baked in: changing it requires a rebuild and a redeploy.
#
# ############################################################################
# #  THE VALUE BELOW IS A PLACEHOLDER. A PRODUCTION BUILD MADE WITH IT AS-IS  #
# #  POINTS AT NOTHING — EVERY API CALL FAILS. TWO THINGS MUST BE DONE:       #
# ############################################################################
#
# vite.config.js REFUSES to build while the placeholder is here, so forgetting
# step 1 fails the GitHub Action instead of rsyncing a broken site over the live
# one. Nothing enforces step 2 — that one is on you.
#
# 1) HERE — set VITE_API_URL to the DEPLOYED ash-ai-backend origin plus
#    /api/storefront. That is the whole storefront API root; src/api/ adds the
#    /v1 itself, so calls land on <origin>/api/storefront/v1/....
#        VITE_API_URL=https://api.reeferclothing.com/api/storefront
#    Must be https — an https page cannot call an http API, the browser blocks it
#    as mixed content. (Ending it at .../api also works; axios.js appends
#    /storefront and never doubles it. Prefer the explicit full form.)
#
# 2) ON THE BACKEND — ash-ai-backend's .env must have
#        STOREFRONT_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://reeferclothing.com
#    (comma-separated; add https://www.reeferclothing.com too if that host is
#    served). Origin only: scheme + host, no path, no trailing slash. Without it
#    the browser blocks EVERY call from the deployed site with a CORS error, and
#    the site looks broken while the API itself is perfectly healthy.
#
VITE_API_URL=https://REPLACE-WITH-YOUR-BACKEND-HOST/api/storefront
